**Proof.**

Let $a_{i,j}$ be the number in the unit square with lower-left corner at $(i,j)$, for all $(i,j)\in\mathbb{Z}^2$.

**Step 1 — Period $n$ in both directions.**

The $1\times n$ rectangle $R_h(i,j)=\{(i,j),(i+1,j),\dots,(i+n-1,j)\}$ is admissible. Its translate $R_h(i+1,j)$ is congruent, so by hypothesis their values are equal:
$$\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}a_{i+k,j}=\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}a_{i+1+k,j}.$$
Subtracting gives $a_{i,j}=a_{i+n,j}$ for all $i,j$: **period $n$ horizontally**. The $n\times 1$ rectangle is congruent to $R_h$ (90° rotation), and the same argument gives $a_{i,j}=a_{i,j+n}$: **period $n$ vertically**.

**Step 2 — Period 2 in both directions via $L$-shapes.**

Consider the $L$-polyomino $L(i,j)=\{(i,j),(i+1,j),\dots,(i+n-2,j),(i,j+1)\}$ of area $n$. Reflecting across the vertical line $x=i$ and translating by $(n-2,0)$ gives $L'(i,j)=\{(i,j),(i+1,j),\dots,(i+n-2,j),(i+n-2,j+1)\}$, which is congruent to $L$ (reflection is a congruence). Their common cells are the bar $\{(i,j),\dots,(i+n-2,j)\}$, so equal values yield
$$a_{i,j+1}=a_{i+n-2,j+1}\quad\text{for all }i,j.$$
Using period $n$: $a_{i+n-2,j+1}=a_{i-2,j+1}$, hence $a_{i,j+1}=a_{i-2,j+1}$: **period 2 horizontally**.

By the analogous argument with the vertical $L$-shape $V(i,j)=\{(i,j),(i,j+1),\dots,(i,j+n-2),(i+1,j)\}$ and its horizontal reflection, we obtain $a_{i,j}=a_{i,j-2}$: **period 2 vertically**.

**Step 3 — Odd $n$: immediate conclusion.**

If $n$ is odd, the grid has periods $n$ and $2$ in each direction, hence period $\gcd(n,2)=1$. All $a_{i,j}$ are equal. $\square$

**Step 4 — Even $n\ge 4$: reduction to a $2\times 2$ block.**

The grid is determined by four values:
$$a=a_{0,0},\quad b=a_{1,0},\quad c=a_{0,1},\quad d=a_{1,1}.$$

*Relating $b,c$ and $a,d$:* A horizontal $1\times n$ strip at even $y$ has value $\frac{n}{2}(a+b)$; at odd $y$ it has value $\frac{n}{2}(c+d)$. These are congruent (translate by 1 in $y$), so $a+b=c+d$. Vertical strips similarly give $a+c=b+d$. Subtracting: $b-c=c-b$, so $b=c$; then $a+b=b+d$ gives $a=d$.

*Relating $a$ and $b$:* With $c=b$ and $d=a$, the value of any admissible polygon $P$ equals $\alpha(P)\cdot a+\beta(P)\cdot b$, where
$$\alpha(P)=|\{(x,y)\in P : x\equiv y\pmod 2\}|,\qquad \beta(P)=n-\alpha(P).$$

Consider the polyomino $T=\{(0,0),(1,0),\dots,(n-2,0),(1,1)\}$ — a horizontal bar of length $n-1$ with a bump at $x=1$. It has area $n$ and is admissible. Its translate $T'=T+(1,0)$ is congruent to $T$.

**Computing $\alpha(T)$:** The bar $\{(x,0):0\le x\le n-2\}$ (with $n-2$ even) has $n/2$ cells with $x$ even ($x+y$ even) and $n/2-1$ with $x$ odd. The bump $(1,1)$ has $x+y=2$, even. So $\alpha(T)=\frac{n}{2}+1$, $\beta(T)=\frac{n}{2}-1$.

**Computing $\alpha(T')$:** The bar $\{(x,0):1\le x\le n-1\}$ (with $n-1$ odd) has $n/2-1$ cells with $x$ even and $n/2$ with $x$ odd. The bump $(2,1)$ has $x+y=3$, odd. So $\alpha(T')=\frac{n}{2}-1$, $\beta(T')=\frac{n}{2}+1$.

Equal values of the congruent polygons $T$ and $T'$ give:
$$\left(\tfrac{n}{2}+1\right)a+\left(\tfrac{n}{2}-1\right)b=\left(\tfrac{n}{2}-1\right)a+\left(\tfrac{n}{2}+1\right)b,$$
so $2a=2b$, hence $a=b$.

Combined with $b=c$ and $a=d$, all four values are equal, so every $a_{i,j}$ is equal. $\square$

This completes the proof for all $n>2$. $\blacksquare$

